<%# §8'S BOTTOM HALF: suggestions, always running, derived from entry history. Local: `presenter`.

    THIS SECTION IS WHY THE PAGE IS PERMANENT RATHER THAN A WIZARD. Two of the four kinds — drift
    and a dead rule — are about rules that already exist and have quietly stopped matching what the
    user actually spends, and a one-time setup can never catch either.

    THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO DISMISS CONTROL (spec §8). Dismissal is state, and the state it would
    hide is drift; the alternative — resurfacing after N periods — is nagging with extra machinery.
    A suggestion leaves this list by being ACTED ON: write the rule and the engine stops proposing
    it on the next load, because the item now carries one.

    EVERY suggestion the engine returns is rendered, in the engine's own order (per-period cost,
    not sticker size) — nothing is truncated here, because a panel that hid the cheapest half
    would be a dismissal with no button. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-suggestions>
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">What your spending suggests</h3>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
      Read from your entries every time this page loads. Nothing here changes anything until you accept it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <% if presenter.suggestions.empty? %>
    <%# THE EMPTY STATE, and it is a real answer rather than a gap: the detectors ran and found
        nothing. A brand-new user with no entries at all lands here too, and so does a user whose
        every bill already has a rule — which is the state this panel exists to get them to. %>
    <div class="px-6 py-8 text-center">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
        Nothing to suggest — every bill and rate we can see in your entries already has a rule.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <%# THE INDEX (Task 7's review). On the demo this panel is about 5,000px tall, and §8 forbids
        both of the usual answers: no truncation (a panel that hid the cheapest half would be a
        dismissal with no button) and no dismissal (the state it would hide is drift). What is left
        is NAVIGATION — say how many of each kind there are and let the reader jump — which hides
        nothing at all. Every suggestion below is still rendered, in the engine's own order.

        The counts and the headings come from ONE grouping (`suggestions_by_kind`), so a count in
        this strip cannot disagree with the run of rows it points at. The engine sorts by kind
        first, so each run is contiguous by construction and this adds no ordering of its own. %>
    <nav class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-sm"
         aria-label="Suggestions by kind"
         data-suggestions-index>
      <% presenter.suggestions_by_kind.each_with_index do |(kind, group), index| %>
        <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true"><%= "·" unless index.zero? %></span>
        <%= link_to suggestion_kind_count(kind, group.size),
                    "##{suggestion_kind_anchor(kind)}",
                    class: "text-brand-dark hover:text-brand underline",
                    data: { suggestions_index_link: kind } %>
      <% end %>
    </nav>

    <% presenter.suggestions_by_kind.each do |kind, group| %>
      <%# The anchor target and the run's own heading are one element: a bare `<span id>` would be
          a landing spot with nothing at it, and a reader who jumped would have to infer from the
          rows what they had jumped to. `scroll-mt-4` keeps the heading off the very top edge of
          the viewport after the jump. %>
      <div id="<%= suggestion_kind_anchor(kind) %>"
           class="px-6 py-2 bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100 scroll-mt-4"
           data-suggestions-group="<%= kind %>">
        <%# Sentence case, not the `uppercase` treatment this codebase gives short labels like
            "Current Balance": "RULES THAT HAVE DRIFTED" shouts a phrase, which is the objection
            home/_attention.html.erb already records about its own band heading. It also made the
            rendered text differ from the markup, so an assertion about the words failed against a
            page that was showing them. %>
        <h4 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-gray-500">
          <%= suggestion_kind_heading(kind) %> · <%= group.size %>
        </h4>
      </div>

      <% group.each do |suggestion| %>
        <%= render "budget_page/suggestion", suggestion: suggestion, presenter: presenter %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</section>
